      <floats-group id="article-21555.s17">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Seddon and Sunderland Classifications of Nerve Injury. This table presents the classifications of nerve injury according to Seddon and Sunderland, correlating each type with the microanatomy involved and the expected degree of recovery. Injuries below the red line are associated with Wallerian degeneration. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Screenshot__2026-04-05_V3" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s18">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>The Facial Nerve. This figure depicts the plan of the facial and intermediate nerves and their communication with other nerves. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=gray+plate+788&#x00026;title=Special:MediaSearch&#x00026;go=Go&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray788" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s19">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Botulinum Toxin injection for Facial Synkinesis. Common targets include the orbicularis oculi, zygomaticus major, buccinator, depressor anguli oris, mentalis, platysma, and the contralateral depressor labii inferioris, which helps reduce lower lip crookedness during smiling. Contributed by TA Hadlock, MD; the Sir Charles Bell Society</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="BotoxMapExample" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s20">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Acute Bell Palsy with a Severe Presentation. Patient with House-Brackmann grade VI. Note the resting effacement of the left nasolabial fold, laxity of the left lower eyelid at rest, and the Bell phenomenon in the left eye. There is no movement of the left hemiface except for transmitted traction from right-sided movements. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <media xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Bells__Acute.mp4" mime-subtype="mp4" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s21">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Post-Bell Palsy Synkinesis. Patient after House-Brackmann grade VI paralysis. Note the essentially symmetric resting appearance (with slight deepening of the left nasolabial fold). There is involuntary movement of the midface and platysma with forceful eye closure and involuntary eye closure with lip puckering. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <media xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Bells__Chronic__Synkinesis.mp4" mime-subtype="mp4" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s22">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f6" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Bell Phenomenon. This image demonstrates Bell phenomenon in a 34-year-old male with House-Brackmann grade VI Bell palsy. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Slide1" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s23">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f7" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Stretching the Upper Eyelid. Stretching the upper eyelid for 60 seconds 2-3 times daily can enhance gravity-assisted eye closure and help prevent exposure keratopathy in patients with facial paralysis. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <media xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Eyelid__Stretching.mp4" mime-subtype="mp4" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s24">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f8" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Taping the Eyelid. Taping the eyelid closed at night helps prevent exposure keratopathy and corneal abrasion from paralytic lagophthalmos. In some cases, taping during the day may also be beneficial. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <media xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Eyelid__Taping.mp4" mime-subtype="mp4" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s25">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f9" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Pretarsal Upper Eyelid Weight Placement. A) The incision is made in the supratarsal crease. B) Dissection is performed through the orbicularis oculi muscle until the tarsal plate is identified. C) The weight is placed into a tight pocket parallel to and 2 mm superior to the inferior edge of the tarsal plate. Hemostasis is achieved, and the weight is sutured in place with 6-0 polypropylene. D) The wound is closed in layers, approximating the muscle and then the skin. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Slide3" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s26">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f10" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Nerve Fascicle and Connective Tissue Layers. In the peripheral nervous system, nerve fibers are organized into fascicles, which are surrounded and supported by connective tissue layers, including the epineurium, perineurium, and endoneurium. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gray636.png">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray636" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s27">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f11" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Masseteric Nerve Transfer. A) the buccal branch controlling the zygomaticus major muscle is identified at Zuker's point (halfway between the helical root and the oral commissure), B) the masseteric nerve is identified 3 cm anterior to the tragus, 1 cm inferior to the zygomatic arch, and 1.5 cm deep to the masseteric fascia; C) the 2 nerves are typically located within ~1.5 cm of each other, which obviates the need for an interposition graft; D) the completed nerve transfer, using 10-0 nylon epineurial sutures to coapt the proximal stump of the masseteric nerve to the distal stump of the buccal branch. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="MassetericNerveTransfer" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s28">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f12" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Hypoglossal Nerve Transfer. A) the main trunk of the facial nerve (red vessel loop) is identified between the stylomastoid foramen and the pes anserinus, and the hypoglossal nerve (blue vessel loop) is located deep to the intermediate tendon of the digastric muscle and superficial to the carotid bifurcation; B) an end-to-side neurorrhaphy is performed between the facial nerve and an interposition graft, C) an end-to-side neurorrhaphy is performed between the hypoglossal nerve and an interposition graft, D) the completed hypoglossal-facial nerve transfer with an interposition graft running from the hypoglossal nerve (blue arrow) to the facial nerve (red arrow). Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS, and AG Vincent, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="HypoglossalNerveTransfer" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s29">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f13" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Management of Acute Bell Palsy. This diagram depicts an algorithm for managing acute Bell palsy based on the time of symptom onset before presentation. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS, and TA Hadlock, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="BellsAlgorithm" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s30">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f14" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Management of Traumatic Facial Paralysis&#x000a0;Algorithm&#x000a0; Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Traumatic__Facial__Palsy" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s31">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f15" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Direct Brow Lift. After marking, the incision is made with a #15 blade (A). The skin is then excised down to the level of the frontalis muscle, taking care to avoid injury to the supraorbital neurovascular bundle (B). A deep tacking suture that suspends the eyebrow's dermis to the forehead's periosteum is optional (C). Closure is completed in 2 layers: deep dermal and skin surface (D). Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Slide2" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s32">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f16" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Rhytidectomy Complications. The image depicts lower lip asymmetry after rhytidectomy due to injury to the nerve that controls the depressor labii inferioris muscle. The left side (arrow) is affected; the patient cannot depress her lower lip on that side. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="LowerLip" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s33">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f17" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Transverse Temporal Bone Fracture. Coronal (a) and axial (b) CT images demonstrate a transverse fracture through the labyrinthine segment of the facial nerve canal (arrows). Axial CT image (c) shows the fracture involving the otic capsule and basal turn of the cochlea (arrowhead). Note blood products in the middle ear cavity (asterisks). Gupta S, Mends F, Hagiwara M, Fatterpekar G, Roehm PC. Imaging the facial nerve: a contemporary review. <italic toggle="yes">Radiol Res Pract</italic>. 2013;2013:248039.&#x000a0;doi:&#x000a0;10.1155/2013/248039.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="t-bone-facial__nerve" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s34">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f18" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Ramsay Hunt Syndrome. Vesicular rash extending to the auricle with facial droop from Ramsay Hunt Syndrome. Contributed&#x000a0;by A Crouch, DO</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="IMG_0193__2" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s35">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f19" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Left-Sided Bell Palsy, MRI. On the patient's right side, there is normal physiological enhancement of the facial nerve due to its epineurial and perineurial blood vessels. Normal enhancement may be seen in the geniculate ganglion, tympanic segment, and mastoid segment of the facial nerve. On the patient's left side, the enhancement is stronger than on the right and extends into the labyrinthine segment and the fundus of the internal auditory canal. Enhancement of the facial nerve in the labyrinthine segment, cerebellopontine angle, internal auditory canal, or parotid gland is abnormal. Contributed by J Costello, DO</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="MRIBells" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s36">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f20" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Congenital Unilateral Lower Lip Palsy. Left:&#x000a0;A 4-month-old infant with a notably asymmetric lower lip&#x02014;the left side is not able to depress as much as the right. Careful examination reveals that the left eye is more open and the left nasolabial fold is more vertical than the right, indicating weakness in the midfacial musculature as well. Right: The same girl 4 months later, demonstrating spontaneous resolution of the facial paralysis. Contributed by COM Hohman and MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="CULLP" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s37">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f21" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Congenital Facial Paralysis. Congenital facial paralysis in a 63-year-old man. During the top-to-bottom systematic facial nerve examination, there is a lack of function on the left side until the orbicularis oris is assessed, below which all functions are intact. Unilateral regional flaccidity of the mid- and upper face is a common presentation for nonsyndromic congenital facial paralysis. Left eye closure is achieved with a gold weight. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <media xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="CorrellPreOp.mp4" mime-subtype="mp4" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s38">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f22" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>The eFACE Software Application. Screenshots of the Massachusetts Eye &#x00026; Ear Infirmary eFACE software used on a tablet computer. The app is a validated instrument that permits quantification of the function and symmetry of individual facial features at rest, during voluntary movement, and during involuntary movement. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="eFACE" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s39">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f23" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Static Facial Suspension With Fascia Lata. The fascia lata is split into three ribbons, with the superior one lateralizing the nasal base to open the external nasal valve and improve the nasal airway, the middle one suspending the midface to recreate the effaced nasolabial fold, and the inferior one suspending the oral commissure to decrease smiling asymmetry and improve oral competence. All three ribbons are tunneled under the facial skin and anchored to the temporalis fascia. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="FasciaLata" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s40">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f24" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Acoustic Neuroma. A 26-year-old male with an acoustic neuroma. Note the extension from the internal auditory canal into the cerebellopontine angle with brainstem compression. Resection of this 5 cm tumor resulted in facial paralysis and hearing loss on the right side. Contributed by MH Hohman, MD, FACS, and DS Ruhl, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="AcousticAlbone" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-21555.s41">
        <fig id="article-21555.image.f25" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Lyme Disease "Bulls-Eye" Rash. This image illustrates the characteristic rash present in some cases of Lyme disease. James Gathany, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://phil.cdc.gov/Details.aspx?pid=9874">Public Domain</ext-link>, Public Health Image Library, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention&#x000a0;</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Target__lesion" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
